	(text "Supply: 48 V DC nominal, the same bus that feeds the CANopen servo drives (DSY-400C: 24-70 V).\nWorking range 34 V to 58 V. Board load is about 30 W at full 5 V output (0.65 A at 48 V, 0.9 A at 34 V).\n\nF1  5x20 mm fuse in a PCB holder (XC-7, LCSC C3131). The fuse is fitted by hand, not by the assembler.\n    Fuse: Littelfuse 0477002.MXP (LCSC C187634): 2 A time-lag, ceramic, 400 VDC, breaks 1500 A at 400 VDC.\n    Any substitute must be ceramic with a DC rating of at least 60 V. Glass 5x20 fuses are usually AC only.\n    (The ATO blade fuses of the 12 V board are 32 V parts and must not be used here.)\nD30 SS510C, 100 V 5 A Schottky in series: blocks a reversed supply. Drops about 0.5 V (0.4 W at 0.8 A).\n    A reversed supply puts 48 V across D30, or up to 48 V + the charge left on C90 if the board was\n    running seconds earlier. Keep that below 100 V: do not hot-swap the polarity.\nD31 SMCJ58A, 1.5 kW TVS: 58 V stand-off, 64.4 V minimum breakdown, 93.6 V clamp at 16 A. It keeps supply\n    surges below the 100 V rating of C90-C97, Q5/Q6 and U23. It is NOT a brake clamp: if the servo drives\n    regenerate into a supply that cannot absorb it and the bus rises above about 64 V, D31 conducts\n    continuously and F1 blows. Fit brake resistors on the drives if the bus can pump up.\nC90 100 uF 100 V electrolytic: bulk capacitance, and its ESR damps the ringing of the supply cable\n    inductance against the ceramic input capacitors of the buck when the supply is plugged in live.\nFB3 120 Ω at 100 MHz bead: keeps the buck switching noise off the 48 V bus and the servo drive noise\n    out of the buck. Carries the full input current (0.9 A max).\n\nThere is no motor current on this board. The servo drives are wired straight to the 48 V supply, so\nthe PGND / NT1 split of the RoboClaw board is gone: J1 pin 2 is board GND."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 20.32 106.68 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "186ea055-1511-4795-bb7f-94b366852106")
	)
	(text "Fuse, reverse-polarity diode, surge clamp, filter"
